# shellcheck shell=sh
#
# Sourced by `uwsm aux prepare-env` (via /usr/libexec/uwsm/prepare-env.sh)
# before the compositor starts. systemd does not read this file and neither does
# your shell; home/apps.nix places this file at ~/.config/uwsm/env via
# xdg.configFile, which is where uwsm looks. POSIX sh, not bash -- prepare-env.sh
# runs /bin/sh.
#
# ~/.nix-profile/bin on PATH.
#
# This block used to name ~/.local/bin, because that is where the old install.sh
# symlinked calango-open, code and apply-gtk-theme. Home Manager puts them in
# ~/.nix-profile/bin instead, and the job moved with them rather than going away
# -- which is not how it looked at first. Both .desktop entries now carry
# absolute store paths, so the portal no longer needs PATH to find the browser
# handler; that much was right. What it missed is that two of the three shims
# are meant to be *typed*.
#
# Nothing else puts the Nix profile on PATH on this machine. There is no
# /etc/profile.d/nix.sh here, and nothing sources
# ~/.nix-profile/etc/profile.d/hm-session-vars.sh, so without this block the
# login PATH is ~/.cargo/bin:~/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin and the
# compositor prepends only home/session.nix's compositorPath on top of it.
# Measured, after a switch that did not have this block:
#
#   foot, lf, qs      -> the Nix builds      (compositorPath)
#   code              -> /usr/bin/code       WRONG, no --use-angle=vulkan
#   calango-open      -> not found
#   apply-gtk-theme   -> not found
#
# `code` is the one that matters: bin/code exists precisely so a terminal launch
# and a launcher launch land on the same renderer, and without this block they
# diverge silently -- one window on radv, another on radeonsi, with nothing
# anywhere saying so. That is the bug that shim was written to kill.
#
# Prepended, so the shims win over /usr/bin/code. The guard makes re-sourcing
# idempotent; the colon padding makes the match exact, so a PATH already holding
# ~/.nix-profile/binx cannot satisfy it; and the quotes keep the pattern literal,
# so a $HOME containing a glob character cannot turn it into a wildcard.
#
# This reaches the systemd user manager too -- prepare-env.sh ends with
# `exec env -0`, and uwsm imports the result -- which is what puts the shims in
# reach of anything the portal or a user unit spawns later.
case ":$PATH:" in
  *:"$HOME/.nix-profile/bin":*) ;;
  *) PATH="$HOME/.nix-profile/bin:$PATH"; export PATH ;;
esac

# Do not point GTK and Qt at an ibus that is not running.
#
# There is no input method on this machine. ../autostart/im-launch.desktop
# switches ibus off and says why -- it segfaulted six times in three weeks, in
# both X11 and Wayland modes, and its only engine duplicated the XKB layout.
#
# So these two must go. GTK_IM_MODULE and QT_IM_MODULE tell each toolkit to load
# an ibus client library and route text input through it. With no ibus daemon
# there is nothing at the other end. GTK falls back quietly, Qt is less
# predictable, and neither has any reason to try. Unset, both toolkits use the
# Wayland text-input protocol their backends already speak.
#
# This block outlived its first purpose. It was written to satisfy ibus's own
# notification, which asks that these two be unset before its Wayland input
# method is configured. That input method is gone; the unset matters more
# without it than with it.
#
# XMODIFIERS and CLUTTER_IM_MODULE stay. Nothing reads them once ibus is absent,
# and leaving them alone keeps this file to the two variables that would
# actually change how a running application behaves.
#
# These are unset rather than never set, because something else sets them first.
# On Debian /etc/profile.d/im-config_wayland.sh sources
# /etc/X11/Xsession.d/70im-config_launch, which exports both. prepare-env.sh
# sources /etc/profile at line 159 and this file later, from load_wm_env -- so
# by the time we run they exist, and the order is what lets us undo them. It
# ends with `exec env -0`, printing the environment it finishes with; a variable
# absent there is never exported to the systemd user manager. Verified on a live
# session: unset here, gone from `systemctl --user show-environment`.
#
# Harmless where the cause is absent. Fedora has no im-config and no
# /etc/profile.d/im-config_wayland.sh, so on epiphany these are already unset
# and `unset` on an unset variable is a no-op that returns 0.
unset GTK_IM_MODULE QT_IM_MODULE
